    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Term-by-term differentiation and integration\n\nInside the disk of convergence $|x - a| < R$, a power series can be differentiated and integrated term-by-term, with the same radius:\n\n$$f(x) = \\sum c_n (x-a)^n \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; f'(x) = \\sum n c_n (x-a)^{n-1}, \\quad \\int f = \\sum \\frac{c_n}{n+1}(x-a)^{n+1} + C.$$\n\nDemonstrate symbolically by integrating $1/(1-x) = \\sum x^n$:\n\n$$\\int \\frac{1}{1-x}\\,dx = -\\log(1-x) = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}.$$\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "log_series : taylor(-log(1 - x), x, 0, 10)$\nprint(\"Taylor series of -log(1-x):\"); log_series;\nprint()$\nprint(\"from term-by-term integration:\")$\nprint(\"  ∫ Σ x^n dx = Σ x^{n+1}/(n+1) = x + x²/2 + x³/3 + ... ✓\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 26,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Taylor series of -log(1-x):\n\"Taylor series of -log(1-x):\"\nx+x^2/2+x^3/3+x^4/4+x^5/5+x^6/6+x^7/7+x^8/8+x^9/9+x^10/10\nfrom term-by-term integration:\n  ∫ Σ x^n dx = Σ x^{n+1}/(n+1) = x + x²/2 + x³/3 + ... ✓"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Why $1/(1+x^2)$ has radius 1 even though it's smooth on $\\mathbb{R}$\n\nReal-analytically, $1/(1+x^2)$ is $C^\\infty$ everywhere — no singularities anywhere on the real line. Yet its Taylor series at 0 has radius **1**.\n\nThe reason is **complex**: $1 + z^2 = 0$ at $z = \\pm i$, distance 1 from the origin in the complex plane. The radius of convergence equals the distance from the centre to the nearest singularity — *in $\\mathbb{C}$*, not $\\mathbb{R}$. This is the simplest hint that real analysis is incomplete; the natural setting for power series is the complex plane (Stage 3).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "x_atan : np_to_list(np_linspace(-1.5, 1.5, 400))$\ntruth_atan : map(lambda([x], 1.0 / (1 + x*x)), x_atan)$\nP_atan : []$\nfor x in x_atan do block([s, k],\n  s : 0.0,\n  for k : 0 thru 30 do s : s + (-1)^k * x^(2*k),\n  P_atan : endcons(s, P_atan))$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"black\", line_width=2, name=\"1/(1+x²) (truth)\",\n  lines(x_atan, truth_atan),\n  color=\"red\", line_width=1, name=\"Σ_{k=0}^{30} (-1)^k x^{2k}\",\n  lines(x_atan, P_atan),\n  color=\"green\", dash=\"dash\", name=\"|x|=1 boundary\",\n  points([1, 1], [-1, 2]),\n  color=\"green\", dash=\"dash\",\n  points([-1, -1], [-1, 2]),\n  title=\"Taylor series of 1/(1+x²) — radius 1 because of complex singularities at ±i\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"value\",\n  grid=true, showlegend=true, yrange=[-1, 2]\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 27,
      "outputs": [
