    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## $C^\\infty$ but not analytic\n\nSmoothness ($C^\\infty$) is strictly weaker than analyticity. The textbook example:\n\n$$f(x) = \\begin{cases} e^{-1/x^2} & x \\ne 0 \\\\ 0 & x = 0 \\end{cases}$$\n\nis $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb{R}$, with $f^{(n)}(0) = 0$ for *every* $n$. So its Taylor series at 0 is identically zero — converges everywhere, but only equals $f$ at the single point $x = 0$.\n\nThis is impossible in complex analysis: a holomorphic function that's $C^\\infty$ at $z = 0$ with all derivatives zero is identically zero on a neighbourhood. Smoothness in $\\mathbb{R}$ is much more permissive than holomorphy in $\\mathbb{C}$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "f_pathological(x) := if x = 0 then 0.0 else exp(-1/(x*x))$\nx_p : np_to_list(np_linspace(-1, 1, 1000))$\ny_p : map(f_pathological, x_p)$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"purple\", line_width=2, name=\"f(x) = e^{-1/x²}\",\n  lines(x_p, y_p),\n  color=\"black\", dash=\"dash\", name=\"Taylor series at 0 (≡ 0)\",\n  explicit(0, t, -1, 1),\n  title=\"C^∞ but not analytic — Taylor series misses the function entirely\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"f(x)\",\n  grid=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 28,
      "outputs": [
        {
