    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Where this leaves us\n\nReal analysis takes us this far:\n\n- Sequences and limits — the bedrock.\n- Continuity, differentiability, integrability — well understood for individual functions.\n- Series of functions — uniform convergence is the key technical tool.\n- Power series — analytic functions sit at the top of the regularity hierarchy.\n\nTwo unfinished threads:\n\n- The smooth-but-not-analytic pathology, the radius of convergence dictated by complex singularities, and the fundamental difference between $C^\\infty$ and holomorphy — all point to **complex analysis** (Stage 3) as the natural completion of the theory of power series.\n- Working in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ (Stage 2) opens up multivariable calculus, vector fields, and the integral theorems (Green, Stokes, divergence) that unify ideas from physics with those of analysis.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}